  "Novel",
  "The rise and fall of a patent-medicine fortune and a portrait of England from the 1870s to the 1900s — Wells's finest novel.",
  "George Ponderevo, nephew of the fraudulent chemist Edward Ponderevo, recounts his uncle's rise from provincial pharmacist to the owner of the Tono-Bungay patent medicine empire, the family's ascent into the wealthy classes, and its collapse. George's other life as an aeronautical engineer and his flight over Africa give the novel its images of the modern age; the book ends with the uncle ruined and dying and England seen as a system of shams.",
  "The patent-medicine boom and the advertising culture of the 1890s, the new wealth of the Edwardian middle class, the failure of English institutions, and the beginnings of aviation.",
  "Tono-Bungay is Wells's masterpiece and one of the great Edwardian condition-of-England novels: it invented the novel of advertising, and its portrait of the sham empire of commerce — the patent medicine as a metaphor for the whole society — is the finest thing Wells wrote.",
