  "Clayhanger is Bennett's most personal novel and the finest English study of the provincial autodidact: it made the Potteries a literary country and gave the nineteenth-century novel of education a modern, ironic form. With Hilda Lessways and These Twain it forms one of the great English family chronicles.",
